<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carpathian Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Carpathians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Carpathian Mountains form an arc about 1,500 km long across central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-gates">Iron Gates</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. They enclose the Pannonian Basin, which the Hungarians entered across their passes in 895. The range has long been a frontier between peoples and states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains.jpg" alt="Carpathian Mountains" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakub Krajňák · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morskie_Oko_in_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
